𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟲/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗘𝗿𝗿𝗼𝗿 𝗛𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 & 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗶𝗹𝗶𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on Error Handling in Ansible — because production automation should not simply fail; it should handle failures intelligently.

❌ 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗛𝗮𝗽𝗽𝗲𝗻𝘀 𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗻 𝗮 𝗧𝗮𝘀𝗸 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝘀?

By default, when a task fails on a host, Ansible stops executing subsequent tasks for that host.

But we can control this behavior.

🛠️ 𝗶𝗴𝗻𝗼𝗿𝗲_𝗲𝗿𝗿𝗼𝗿𝘀

Continue execution even when a task fails:

- name: Run command
  ansible.builtin.command: /some/command
  ignore_errors: true

⚠️ Use this carefully — ignoring every error can hide real problems.

🎯 𝗳𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗱_𝘄𝗵𝗲𝗻

Define your own failure condition:

- name: Check application
  ansible.builtin.command: /usr/local/bin/check_app
  register: result
  failed_when: result.rc != 0

🔄 𝗯𝗹𝗼𝗰𝗸 / 𝗿𝗲𝘀𝗰𝘂𝗲 / 𝗮𝗹𝘄𝗮𝘆𝘀

One of the most useful patterns for structured error handling:

- block:
    - name: Deploy application
      ansible.builtin.command: /opt/deploy.sh

  rescue:
    - name: Handle deployment failure
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Deployment failed!"

  always:
    - name: Cleanup
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Running cleanup..."

𝗯𝗹𝗼𝗰𝗸 → Tasks that may fail
𝗿𝗲𝘀𝗰𝘂𝗲 → What to do if they fail
𝗮𝗹𝘄𝗮𝘆𝘀 → Runs regardless of success or failure

🚨 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗘𝗿𝗿𝗼𝗿 𝗛𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗠𝗮𝘁𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘀

In real DevOps environments, deployments can fail because of:

🔌 Network issues
💾 Disk problems
🔐 Permission issues
📦 Package failures
⚙️ Application errors

Proper error handling helps automation recover, report failures clearly, and perform cleanup instead of leaving systems in an unexpected state.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

𝗥𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗮𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝗶𝘀𝗻'𝘁 𝗮𝗯𝗼𝘂𝘁 𝗮𝘃𝗼𝗶𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗳𝗮𝗶𝗹𝘂𝗿𝗲𝘀.

It's about handling failures predictably when they happen. 🚀
